edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370458} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb892db-47c0-5c2c-9f28-82b937114122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370459} {:answer/id #uuid "933d9a45-6cc4-5308-bbac-a8a57535238e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370460} {:answer/id #uuid "6e872aac-05ba-58e5-ba5d-e39015c1db21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d93b58c-5231-5a80-86f1-2e9d24fb9b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370458} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a9a8df-f37b-50dd-8556-f70bacf81294" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd854bc5-9792-54c0-806a-73dbdfda7584" :question/math-academy-id "q-335630" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.3x)-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n| $J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=3x(1-0.3x)-4xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x(1-0.3x)-4xy)=3-1.8x-4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x(1-0.3x)-4xy)=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+xy)=-1+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}3-1.8x-4y & -4x \\\\ y & -1+x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}3-1.8(0)-4(0) & -4(0) \\\\ 0 & -1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"}]}
